Multi-Environment GitOps (Dev, Stage, Prod)

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m learning how GitOps is used in real production environments with multiple stages 🚀
This is a must-know concept for real DevOps jobs.

🔹 What is Multi-Environment Setup?
 In real projects, applications are not deployed directly to production.
 They go through multiple environments like:
Dev (Development) → For testing new features
Stage (Staging) → For pre-production validation
Prod (Production) → Live application for users

🔹 How GitOps Manages Multiple Environments
 Using GitOps, each environment can be managed using:
Separate folders (dev/, stage/, prod/)
Separate branches (like dev, main)
Different configuration files (replicas, resources, etc.)
Each environment has its own desired state defined in Git.

🔹 Real Production Workflow
 1️⃣ Developer pushes code → goes to Dev
 2️⃣ After testing → promoted to Stage
 3️⃣ After approval → deployed to Prod
GitOps tools automatically sync each environment based on Git changes.

🔹 Benefits
Safe and controlled releases
Easy tracking of changes
Environment-specific configurations
Reduced risk in production

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps enables structured and safe deployments across multiple environments, just like real-world production systems.
